00:00:38.500 --> 00:00:40.975
at Google I/O, so there'll
be a lot more activity

00:00:40.975 --> 00:00:43.480
in around one month's time.

00:00:43.480 --> 00:00:46.870
So, yeah, I head up the
Android Enterprise engineering

00:00:46.870 --> 00:00:47.770
division.

00:00:47.770 --> 00:00:49.900
And we're actually
based out of London.

00:00:49.900 --> 00:00:52.922
Android is actually
built in two locations.

00:00:52.922 --> 00:00:54.380
Most of it's here
in Mountain View.

00:00:54.380 --> 00:00:57.640
But there are also some
significant engineering teams

00:00:57.640 --> 00:01:00.770
in London as well.

00:01:00.770 --> 00:01:03.798
So today, we're going
to look at privacy.

00:01:03.798 --> 00:01:05.340
I say pr-ih-vacy
because I'm British.

00:01:05.340 --> 00:01:06.963
I tried switching
to pr-i-vacy but I

00:01:06.963 --> 00:01:08.130
couldn't do it consistently.

00:01:08.130 --> 00:01:13.360
So if you don't understand,
privacy equals privacy--

00:01:13.360 --> 00:01:19.450
so how privacy can benefit
IT and employees, and some

00:01:19.450 --> 00:01:21.310
of the unique
features we have in

00:01:21.310 --> 00:01:24.820
Android that we've
introduced recently,

00:01:24.820 --> 00:01:27.580
and also just to give you
a feeling for where Android

00:01:27.580 --> 00:01:32.170
is going in the enterprise.

00:01:32.170 --> 00:01:36.400
So as you know, privacy has
never been more important.

00:01:36.400 --> 00:01:38.280
Not only has the legal
landscape changed,

00:01:38.280 --> 00:01:41.700
with things like GDPR and
so on, but also people's

00:01:41.700 --> 00:01:46.770
attitudes towards
technology and privacy

00:01:46.770 --> 00:01:49.680
as technology plays a
increasingly important role

00:01:49.680 --> 00:01:52.750
in people's lives.

00:01:52.750 --> 00:01:56.880
So when we talk to
customers, we consistently

00:01:56.880 --> 00:01:59.640
hear about this
tension between secure

00:01:59.640 --> 00:02:03.090
and always-on and
private and the ability

00:02:03.090 --> 00:02:05.330
to detach from work.

00:02:05.330 --> 00:02:09.330
And this is exaggerated
in BYOD devices

00:02:09.330 --> 00:02:15.030
where users have a greater
expectation of privacy.

00:02:15.030 --> 00:02:16.833
And it's in this
tension that we felt

00:02:16.833 --> 00:02:18.250
there is an
opportunity in Android

00:02:18.250 --> 00:02:25.190
for IT to enable security whilst
also enabling employee privacy,

00:02:25.190 --> 00:02:27.235
especially BYOD devices.

00:02:30.250 --> 00:02:32.590
So you could ask, is
this just a problem

00:02:32.590 --> 00:02:34.870
that the Valley has
and we're solving it

00:02:34.870 --> 00:02:37.510
for other people in the Valley?

00:02:37.510 --> 00:02:40.150
Is this some kind of
touchy-feely problem?

00:02:40.150 --> 00:02:43.690
Or is there a real opportunity
in actually solving

00:02:43.690 --> 00:02:45.490
the privacy problem?

00:02:45.490 --> 00:02:49.210
When we're talking to customers,
privacy consent consistently

00:02:49.210 --> 00:02:52.780
comes up as the number-one
reason why mobile deployments

00:02:52.780 --> 00:02:57.180
hit friction, meaning
adoption of BYOD

00:02:57.180 --> 00:02:59.382
isn't what companies expect.

00:02:59.382 --> 00:03:00.840
And this is also
shown on the slide

00:03:00.840 --> 00:03:05.490
here in the 2018 IDC survey.

00:03:05.490 --> 00:03:06.960
Android actually
has a unique mode

00:03:06.960 --> 00:03:08.610
to deal with this very issue.

00:03:08.610 --> 00:03:11.190
And it's one of my favorite
features in Android.

00:03:11.190 --> 00:03:14.670
So we'll come on
to that shortly.

00:03:14.670 --> 00:03:17.790
So Android is helping companies
make mobility deployments

00:03:17.790 --> 00:03:19.240
successful.

00:03:19.240 --> 00:03:22.830
We want to change the dynamic
between employees and IT.

00:03:22.830 --> 00:03:27.120
Employees should feel empowered
and not burdened by technology

00:03:27.120 --> 00:03:30.870
with something that is simple,
secure, and respectful.

00:03:30.870 --> 00:03:32.760
But we also want IT
to feel empowered,

00:03:32.760 --> 00:03:37.050
to increase privacy and
well-being without compromising

00:03:37.050 --> 00:03:41.810
on security by decoupling
these three concepts.

00:03:41.810 --> 00:03:45.570
And so with some of
Android's unique offerings,

00:03:45.570 --> 00:03:51.020
IT decision makers can actually
advocate for their employees.

00:03:51.020 --> 00:03:55.420
So this is apparent dichotomy
between security and privacy.

00:03:55.420 --> 00:03:58.060
Can these truly
be separated such

00:03:58.060 --> 00:04:03.260
that having strong security
does not mean less privacy?

00:04:03.260 --> 00:04:05.060
Well, let's take a look.

00:04:05.060 --> 00:04:08.050
So this is the typical world
of enterprise technology.

00:04:08.050 --> 00:04:10.420
Patch Tuesday marked
on the calendar

00:04:10.420 --> 00:04:13.180
gives IT the best chance of
ensuring devices are secure,

00:04:13.180 --> 00:04:15.910
and running virus scans, and
monitoring everything which

00:04:15.910 --> 00:04:19.959
is happening on the
device continuously using,

00:04:19.959 --> 00:04:22.120
typically, third-party software.

00:04:22.120 --> 00:04:26.330
Security is actually
enabled through visibility.

00:04:26.330 --> 00:04:29.150
IT needs to know about
everything happening

00:04:29.150 --> 00:04:35.510
on the device in order to ensure
nothing insecure is happening.

00:04:35.510 --> 00:04:37.910
Now, mobile is
increasingly shifting away

00:04:37.910 --> 00:04:41.600
from this philosophy using
some emerging new security

00:04:41.600 --> 00:04:43.550
capabilities.

00:04:43.550 --> 00:04:48.100
Yet a lot of assumptions around
security through visibility

00:04:48.100 --> 00:04:49.210
have carried on to mobile.

00:04:49.210 --> 00:04:51.740
You know, OK, what's
the virus scanning

00:04:51.740 --> 00:04:53.060
solution for mobile phones?

00:04:53.060 --> 00:04:55.490
And how do I get visibility
into everything which

00:04:55.490 --> 00:04:56.615
is happening on the device?

00:05:00.630 --> 00:05:02.510
We don't think that
privacy needs to be

00:05:02.510 --> 00:05:06.260
at the cost of great security.

00:05:06.260 --> 00:05:09.050
We also don't believe device
ownership should determine

00:05:09.050 --> 00:05:10.880
how secure a device can be.

00:05:10.880 --> 00:05:14.330
There's no universal
law that says you can't

00:05:14.330 --> 00:05:16.170
have a secure BYOD device.

00:05:16.170 --> 00:05:21.078
It's simply down to how
we architect platforms.

00:05:21.078 --> 00:05:22.620
Now, IT have a
challenging enough job

00:05:22.620 --> 00:05:25.180
as it is enabling
business-use cases.

00:05:25.180 --> 00:05:27.180
We don't want IT to have
to worry about plugging

00:05:27.180 --> 00:05:29.665
gaps in the platform itself.

00:05:29.665 --> 00:05:31.290
We also don't want
you to have to worry

00:05:31.290 --> 00:05:33.870
about installing third-party
security solutions

00:05:33.870 --> 00:05:36.720
and worrying about the patch
rate in a particular month

00:05:36.720 --> 00:05:39.440
to ensure devices are secure.

00:05:39.440 --> 00:05:42.010
So we're trying to make sure
that ownership, security,

00:05:42.010 --> 00:05:44.950
and privacy can be
configured individually

00:05:44.950 --> 00:05:49.246
without the need for a business
to go to another vendor.

00:05:49.246 --> 00:05:53.550
So that all sounds nice, but
how do we make sure that--

00:05:53.550 --> 00:05:54.840
how do we do this in practice?

00:05:54.840 --> 00:05:59.490
Well, let's look at some of
the fundamental architecture

00:05:59.490 --> 00:06:02.370
of Android.

00:06:02.370 --> 00:06:06.470
So first of all, we have
server-side malware scanning,

00:06:06.470 --> 00:06:09.590
so through Google
Play and Managed Play.

00:06:09.590 --> 00:06:14.000
So Managed Play is a version of
Google Play which enterprises

00:06:14.000 --> 00:06:17.120
can use to curate
their own app store

00:06:17.120 --> 00:06:18.980
and even push out
apps onto devices,

00:06:18.980 --> 00:06:21.290
update devices, and manage
their configuration.

00:06:21.290 --> 00:06:25.790
So anything that comes through
Google Play or Managed Play,

00:06:25.790 --> 00:06:27.837
first of all, will
be scanned there.

00:06:27.837 --> 00:06:29.420
And then we've got
Google Play Protect

00:06:29.420 --> 00:06:33.670
for device-side
scanning as well.

00:06:33.670 --> 00:06:37.560
Now, Android is actually
built-- or it was originally

00:06:37.560 --> 00:06:39.810
built on top of SELinux.

00:06:39.810 --> 00:06:42.360
And one of the great
benefits of SELinux

00:06:42.360 --> 00:06:45.120
is process isolation,
which means

00:06:45.120 --> 00:06:47.460
that if something
insecure is happening

00:06:47.460 --> 00:06:49.920
in one of these boxes, so
let's say one of these boxes

00:06:49.920 --> 00:06:52.650
was a piece of malware or
somehow some malware got

00:06:52.650 --> 00:06:58.980
injected, well, the blast
radius is more or less impacted

00:06:58.980 --> 00:07:04.320
to the app itself and
not outside of that.

00:07:04.320 --> 00:07:06.030
So this is kind of
process isolation.

00:07:06.030 --> 00:07:08.430
So if a process
becomes compromised,

00:07:08.430 --> 00:07:11.600
it can't gain access to the
space of another process.

00:07:14.707 --> 00:07:16.790
And you can also interrogate
the operating system.

00:07:16.790 --> 00:07:20.770
So you can ask it
questions like,

00:07:20.770 --> 00:07:22.960
is the version of the
operating system that's

00:07:22.960 --> 00:07:25.770
running on this device,
is it a official version?

00:07:25.770 --> 00:07:28.200
And that would be
cryptographically tested

00:07:28.200 --> 00:07:31.312
with a trusted boot using
a hardware root of trust.

00:07:31.312 --> 00:07:32.770
So you can ask
questions like that.

00:07:32.770 --> 00:07:34.480
You can ask simple
questions like,

00:07:34.480 --> 00:07:37.420
is the password on
this device, does it

00:07:37.420 --> 00:07:40.390
meet my criteria for strong?

00:07:40.390 --> 00:07:44.200
Or has Google Play Protect
flagged any issues?

00:07:44.200 --> 00:07:46.600
So two things fall out of this--

00:07:46.600 --> 00:07:49.660
remarkably flexible management
and, for the first time,

00:07:49.660 --> 00:07:54.290
the ability to remove conflict
between privacy and security.

00:07:54.290 --> 00:07:56.380
So you don't need to
know what's going on

00:07:56.380 --> 00:07:58.720
inside one of these
boxes necessarily

00:07:58.720 --> 00:08:01.180
to know that the overall
device is secure.

00:08:04.700 --> 00:08:07.950
But that might not be
enough to convince you.

00:08:07.950 --> 00:08:08.450
Fair enough.

00:08:08.450 --> 00:08:11.280
So let's look at
another feature.

00:08:11.280 --> 00:08:14.430
And this is my favorite
feature in Android,

00:08:14.430 --> 00:08:17.240
and it's used heavily
by Googlers but also

00:08:17.240 --> 00:08:19.280
many other companies,
small and large,

00:08:19.280 --> 00:08:21.620
so something called
Work Profile.

00:08:21.620 --> 00:08:26.120
And the way that I kind
of explain this is it's

00:08:26.120 --> 00:08:29.900
like having two phones
in one piece of hardware.

00:08:29.900 --> 00:08:31.800
So on the right, you
can see on your screen,

00:08:31.800 --> 00:08:33.470
you've got a Work tab.

00:08:33.470 --> 00:08:36.890
And on the left, you've
got a Personal tab.

00:08:36.890 --> 00:08:38.990
Everything that goes on in--

00:08:38.990 --> 00:08:42.200
anything that goes on the
work side of your phone cannot

00:08:42.200 --> 00:08:44.270
access the personal side.

00:08:44.270 --> 00:08:46.970
And anything that happens in
the personal side cannot access

00:08:46.970 --> 00:08:47.930
the work side.

00:08:47.930 --> 00:08:51.080
So not only have you got
these kind of boundaries

00:08:51.080 --> 00:08:53.930
between processes themselves,
which I talked about

00:08:53.930 --> 00:08:55.790
on the previous
slide, but you've also

00:08:55.790 --> 00:08:58.550
got this kind of almost
two virtual phones running

00:08:58.550 --> 00:08:59.420
inside this phone.

00:09:02.230 --> 00:09:06.770
And that separation is
enforced at the OS level, which

00:09:06.770 --> 00:09:09.050
means that multiple
vulnerabilities would have

00:09:09.050 --> 00:09:12.100
to be exploited for something
in a personal profile

00:09:12.100 --> 00:09:15.620
to access anything
in the work profile--

00:09:15.620 --> 00:09:19.040
both application sandboxing
and the OS security

00:09:19.040 --> 00:09:21.530
boundary between
these two areas.

00:09:24.130 --> 00:09:26.905
So in the traditional
world, this would've broken.

00:09:26.905 --> 00:09:28.280
If you can't see
what's happening

00:09:28.280 --> 00:09:29.947
on the personal side
of the phone, well,

00:09:29.947 --> 00:09:32.490
how do you know whether
the device is secure?

00:09:32.490 --> 00:09:35.060
Well, the kind of
Android stance on this

00:09:35.060 --> 00:09:38.900
is it doesn't
matter because what

00:09:38.900 --> 00:09:41.820
happens on the personal side
can't access the work side.

00:09:41.820 --> 00:09:45.010
They're almost like
two separate phones.

00:09:45.010 --> 00:09:48.200
And even from the work side,
you can ask the OS questions

00:09:48.200 --> 00:09:51.100
about the overall security
stance of the device.

00:09:51.100 --> 00:09:58.550
So you can ask it, are there
any insecure or untoward apps

00:09:58.550 --> 00:09:59.990
on this phone anywhere?

00:09:59.990 --> 00:10:02.428
And it will tell you
the answer to that

00:10:02.428 --> 00:10:04.220
by looking in the
personal and work profile

00:10:04.220 --> 00:10:07.320
without breaching the
trust of the user.

00:10:07.320 --> 00:10:10.677
And then you can take action as
a result of that as well, which

00:10:10.677 --> 00:10:12.260
is usually configured
to be automatic,

00:10:12.260 --> 00:10:17.120
like wipe out the work profile.

00:10:17.120 --> 00:10:21.080
And so you get these kind of
strong-level OS assurances

00:10:21.080 --> 00:10:24.350
that the device is in a
good security posture,

00:10:24.350 --> 00:10:28.340
meaning direct visibility is
not needed to ensure security.

00:10:28.340 --> 00:10:31.910
Hence, whatever the user is
doing in their personal profile

00:10:31.910 --> 00:10:33.850
remains private.

00:10:33.850 --> 00:10:35.600
You can't look at
network logs or anything

00:10:35.600 --> 00:10:39.390
like that from
the personal side.

00:10:39.390 --> 00:10:43.880
And yet companies are
able to control and do

00:10:43.880 --> 00:10:46.178
whatever they want within
the work side of the phone.

00:10:46.178 --> 00:10:47.720
And if you really
wanted to put virus

00:10:47.720 --> 00:10:50.095
scanning on there on the work
side, you absolutely could.

00:10:53.220 --> 00:10:57.750
So IT only manages and views
apps in the work profile.

00:10:57.750 --> 00:11:00.120
Personal data is
stored separately.

00:11:00.120 --> 00:11:04.590
IT can erase the work
profile or apps and data.

00:11:04.590 --> 00:11:08.350
It can't view or
erase personal data.

00:11:08.350 --> 00:11:12.180
It's literally like having
two phones in one phone.

00:11:12.180 --> 00:11:16.200
Now, if you're provisioning
a device on BYOD--

00:11:16.200 --> 00:11:18.300
so let's say you bring
your own device to work.

00:11:18.300 --> 00:11:21.780
The typical flow, the
thing that happens

00:11:21.780 --> 00:11:27.100
is you enter your work
email address and password.

00:11:27.100 --> 00:11:28.320
You add an account.

00:11:28.320 --> 00:11:31.110
And if you've configured a
work profile, it will say,

00:11:31.110 --> 00:11:33.420
either you have the option
to have a web profile

00:11:33.420 --> 00:11:37.230
or you must have a web profile
depending on what you set.

00:11:37.230 --> 00:11:40.290
The device will
then automatically

00:11:40.290 --> 00:11:41.910
create that separate
space for you,

00:11:41.910 --> 00:11:44.970
that separate web profile, that
separate phone within a phone.

00:11:47.740 --> 00:11:51.120
So I think the takeaway
from this section

00:11:51.120 --> 00:11:54.520
is you consider
enabling work profile,

00:11:54.520 --> 00:12:00.740
especially for reasons of
privacy for your employees,

00:12:00.740 --> 00:12:05.930
and you'll almost certainly find
that your BYO adoption goes up.

00:12:08.600 --> 00:12:10.780
And so we believe this
is the modern approach

00:12:10.780 --> 00:12:13.900
to mobile security, which
enables privacy and helps

00:12:13.900 --> 00:12:17.000
eliminate the lack of certainty
which was often a deployment

00:12:17.000 --> 00:12:20.380
blocker in a lot of places.

00:12:20.380 --> 00:12:24.610
Employee confidence-- they
know that data belongs to them,

00:12:24.610 --> 00:12:27.140
and they know what their
employer can and can't see.

00:12:27.140 --> 00:12:29.380
And because these are
separate data stores,

00:12:29.380 --> 00:12:32.440
if the employee leaves or
the account is compromised,

00:12:32.440 --> 00:12:35.515
the work data can be wiped
without losing personal data.

00:12:38.210 --> 00:12:40.540
So it reduces the risks
associated with personal data

00:12:40.540 --> 00:12:42.740
loss.

00:12:42.740 --> 00:12:47.010
And because it's clear what
is work and what is personal,

00:12:47.010 --> 00:12:50.580
users don't have this kind of
scary, unknown question around

00:12:50.580 --> 00:12:52.200
whether they're
allowed to be doing

00:12:52.200 --> 00:12:55.740
certain things on the
device or being uncertain

00:12:55.740 --> 00:12:58.430
about what the company may
or may not be able to see.

00:13:01.710 --> 00:13:04.110
So think about the
atmosphere around user data

00:13:04.110 --> 00:13:08.740
privacy, GDPR in the EU.

00:13:08.740 --> 00:13:11.250
This is a chance for the
Android as a platform

00:13:11.250 --> 00:13:14.370
to enable IT to align business
interests with employee

00:13:14.370 --> 00:13:17.490
interests and an opportunity
for IT to get ahead

00:13:17.490 --> 00:13:20.200
of emerging expectations
around this technology.

00:13:23.010 --> 00:13:26.690
So we covered the
privacy/security trade-off.

00:13:26.690 --> 00:13:28.945
But another trade-off
that we talked about,

00:13:28.945 --> 00:13:30.320
this kind of a
double-edged sword

00:13:30.320 --> 00:13:36.740
with always-on connectivity,
that promise that now you

00:13:36.740 --> 00:13:39.350
can work from anywhere, this
kind of mobile workforce

00:13:39.350 --> 00:13:41.930
being the mantra of
enterprise deployments.

00:13:41.930 --> 00:13:44.780
Well, that's great until
it's time not to be working,

00:13:44.780 --> 00:13:46.760
and everything is still
running on your device.

00:13:50.215 --> 00:13:51.590
So let's look at
how we're trying

00:13:51.590 --> 00:13:54.662
to separate the notion of
mobility and always-on.

00:13:57.413 --> 00:13:59.330
We talked to a lot of
users, and the responses

00:13:59.330 --> 00:14:01.500
have been amusing
and concerning.

00:14:04.080 --> 00:14:08.085
So my phone is the first thing
I look at when I wake up.

00:14:08.085 --> 00:14:09.960
I think probably a lot
of people in this room

00:14:09.960 --> 00:14:11.250
can relate to that.

00:14:11.250 --> 00:14:14.130
I certainly can.

00:14:14.130 --> 00:14:15.570
If I don't respond
in 30 minutes,

00:14:15.570 --> 00:14:16.920
people think I've died.

00:14:16.920 --> 00:14:19.482
Actually, this is a true story.

00:14:19.482 --> 00:14:20.940
So someone went
hiking for the day,

00:14:20.940 --> 00:14:25.770
and didn't set their
out-of-office notification

00:14:25.770 --> 00:14:27.060
on their calendar.

00:14:27.060 --> 00:14:29.490
They didn't have
connectivity, and they

00:14:29.490 --> 00:14:32.850
didn't respond to a
message within three hours.

00:14:32.850 --> 00:14:34.770
And their colleagues
called the police

00:14:34.770 --> 00:14:38.040
and reported him as
a missing person.

00:14:38.040 --> 00:14:39.950
It's kind of
ridiculous, isn't it?

00:14:39.950 --> 00:14:44.000
My phone has become a dictator.

00:14:44.000 --> 00:14:47.260
So the device which is supposed
to be enabling us actually

00:14:47.260 --> 00:14:49.120
becomes a ball and chain.

00:14:49.120 --> 00:14:52.594
It's that double-edged sword
of connectivity and well-being.

00:14:55.200 --> 00:14:57.240
So as a society, we
haven't caught up

00:14:57.240 --> 00:14:59.070
with how to manage this.

00:14:59.070 --> 00:15:02.130
And the tools haven't caught up
with human behavior, frankly.

00:15:02.130 --> 00:15:05.160
Users don't have a way
to filter or mitigate

00:15:05.160 --> 00:15:08.900
this always-on reality
we now live in.

00:15:08.900 --> 00:15:11.660
Android is taking this
problem very seriously

00:15:11.660 --> 00:15:15.190
and is starting to address it.

00:15:15.190 --> 00:15:17.960
So we introduced the concept
of digital well-being

00:15:17.960 --> 00:15:20.210
in Android in Google
I/O last spring.

00:15:22.780 --> 00:15:25.960
And the kind of
concept behind this

00:15:25.960 --> 00:15:28.000
is we don't want phone
ruining our lives.

00:15:28.000 --> 00:15:30.700
They should be improving
lives and making workers

00:15:30.700 --> 00:15:33.370
more productive, certainly
not distracting us

00:15:33.370 --> 00:15:35.870
from the most important
things in life.

00:15:35.870 --> 00:15:39.480
So we're starting to introduce
some features to advance this.

00:15:39.480 --> 00:15:42.727
So I previously
introduced Work Profile.

00:15:42.727 --> 00:15:45.060
Now, one of the benefits I
didn't talk about at the time

00:15:45.060 --> 00:15:46.770
was, well, because there's
kind of essentially two

00:15:46.770 --> 00:15:49.410
phones in one phone, you can
switch one of the phones off,

00:15:49.410 --> 00:15:52.630
so you can switch
your work phone off.

00:15:52.630 --> 00:15:57.630
And that means that everything
on there is gone, literally.

00:15:57.630 --> 00:16:01.650
Nothing can drain your
battery in the background.

00:16:01.650 --> 00:16:03.360
It can't sync.

00:16:03.360 --> 00:16:08.490
There's no notifications
from any of your work apps.

00:16:08.490 --> 00:16:10.650
And actually, I use this
feature all the time.

00:16:10.650 --> 00:16:15.220
So late in the evening, I tend
to switch my work profile off.

00:16:15.220 --> 00:16:17.220
And then when I wake up
in the morning with kind

00:16:17.220 --> 00:16:20.050
of like one eye
looking at my phone,

00:16:20.050 --> 00:16:21.990
I don't get a bunch
of work emails.

00:16:21.990 --> 00:16:24.820
I switch that back
on when I'm ready.

00:16:24.820 --> 00:16:27.870
And other people switch that off
at weekends and other things.

00:16:27.870 --> 00:16:31.890
Because we know, now because
we've got this profile,

00:16:31.890 --> 00:16:34.680
we know what are work apps
and what aren't because, well,

00:16:34.680 --> 00:16:37.290
your work apps are in your
work side of your phone.

00:16:37.290 --> 00:16:38.995
You can to switch
that work side off.

00:16:38.995 --> 00:16:40.662
And actually, the
interesting thing-- it

00:16:40.662 --> 00:16:43.240
doesn't work quite
like this animation.

00:16:43.240 --> 00:16:45.740
So when you switch it back on,
because the cryptographic key

00:16:45.740 --> 00:16:48.840
has been thrown away, it will
actually ask you for your pin

00:16:48.840 --> 00:16:52.490
again because, again, it
has to log in to that phone.

00:16:57.820 --> 00:17:02.440
So you've got the kind of work
profile switch, on and off.

00:17:02.440 --> 00:17:04.569
That's one digital
well-being thing.

00:17:04.569 --> 00:17:06.819
We've added the ability
to let you see how

00:17:06.819 --> 00:17:08.650
to spend time on your device.

00:17:08.650 --> 00:17:12.280
By giving user's data,
we can help them become

00:17:12.280 --> 00:17:14.880
more aware of their habits.

00:17:14.880 --> 00:17:17.050
Yeah, often what
happens, to me at least,

00:17:17.050 --> 00:17:21.010
is I get a notification
that an email comes in.

00:17:21.010 --> 00:17:23.780
So I open my phone up
to respond to that.

00:17:23.780 --> 00:17:27.220
And then I get another
notification from social media.

00:17:27.220 --> 00:17:30.345
And before I know it, I've
spent 10 minutes scrolling up

00:17:30.345 --> 00:17:31.720
social media, and
I've completely

00:17:31.720 --> 00:17:36.110
forgotten why I unlocked that
device in the first place.

00:17:36.110 --> 00:17:39.400
So we want to make sure that the
time you spend on your device

00:17:39.400 --> 00:17:42.550
is high-quality time by giving
you visibility and providing

00:17:42.550 --> 00:17:44.760
controls.

00:17:44.760 --> 00:17:46.490
So first, in terms
of awareness, we've

00:17:46.490 --> 00:17:48.550
introduced a dashboard
which gives you

00:17:48.550 --> 00:17:52.810
snapshots of your digital
habits with three metrics.

00:17:52.810 --> 00:17:56.300
So you've got your
screen time, so

00:17:56.300 --> 00:18:03.390
how you spend that, which apps
you are in the most, and so on,

00:18:03.390 --> 00:18:06.920
and then how many times
you've unlocked your phone.

00:18:06.920 --> 00:18:09.080
Often, we don't realize
actually how much

00:18:09.080 --> 00:18:12.410
time, how often we
do that in a day,

00:18:12.410 --> 00:18:17.090
and how many notifications
you receive, so which apps

00:18:17.090 --> 00:18:19.610
are distracting you the most.

00:18:19.610 --> 00:18:22.210
So on the left here, you'll
see how your day breaks down

00:18:22.210 --> 00:18:23.855
on what you spend your time on.

00:18:23.855 --> 00:18:25.480
And if you tap for
further information,

00:18:25.480 --> 00:18:28.060
you can see trends over time.

00:18:28.060 --> 00:18:30.362
And this is especially
useful if you're

00:18:30.362 --> 00:18:31.570
trying to change your habits.

00:18:31.570 --> 00:18:33.160
You can actually
see, well, you know,

00:18:33.160 --> 00:18:36.970
am I actually changing my habits
by looking at these trends?

00:18:36.970 --> 00:18:39.100
And for the apps that you
want to be mindful of,

00:18:39.100 --> 00:18:41.700
we've introduced app timers.

00:18:41.700 --> 00:18:44.260
And so once you've
used the amount of time

00:18:44.260 --> 00:18:45.640
you've allotted
for that, the app

00:18:45.640 --> 00:18:49.622
will gray-out and will be
paused for the rest of the day.

00:18:49.622 --> 00:18:51.580
And you won't get a
notifications from that app

00:18:51.580 --> 00:18:52.080
either.

00:18:57.330 --> 00:19:01.030
So we've all experienced how
distracting these devices

00:19:01.030 --> 00:19:02.530
can be.

00:19:02.530 --> 00:19:06.060
Sitting down in a meeting,
getting a notification,

00:19:06.060 --> 00:19:08.840
often that happens
to several people

00:19:08.840 --> 00:19:12.850
in the meeting and,
of course, the devices

00:19:12.850 --> 00:19:15.460
in this case interacting
with us when we actually

00:19:15.460 --> 00:19:17.910
don't want it to.

00:19:17.910 --> 00:19:19.440
We don't want to be distracted.

00:19:19.440 --> 00:19:20.420
We're in a meeting.

00:19:20.420 --> 00:19:23.900
And so we've
introduced gestures.

00:19:23.900 --> 00:19:26.000
One of them is Flip to Shhh.

00:19:26.000 --> 00:19:30.120
So if you flip your phone
over, it won't distract you.

00:19:30.120 --> 00:19:36.360
It won't make any
notification sounds or ring

00:19:36.360 --> 00:19:39.680
if there are incoming calls.

00:19:39.680 --> 00:19:41.380
So this is on the
latest pixel devices

00:19:41.380 --> 00:19:44.530
and a number of other
devices from various OEMs.

00:19:48.080 --> 00:19:51.160
The other feature we've
introduced is Wind Down.

00:19:51.160 --> 00:19:54.800
So lack of sleep
caused a $400 billion

00:19:54.800 --> 00:19:56.840
productivity hit to
companies in the US

00:19:56.840 --> 00:20:00.110
in 2016, according to a study.

00:20:00.110 --> 00:20:02.690
And for many people in this
room, that's tens of millions

00:20:02.690 --> 00:20:04.560
of dollars.

00:20:04.560 --> 00:20:07.400
So, and we sort of realized
that getting pinged at 11:00

00:20:07.400 --> 00:20:11.756
PM probably isn't
helping with that.

00:20:11.756 --> 00:20:14.030
And because sleep
is really important

00:20:14.030 --> 00:20:15.590
and can keep us
from getting enough,

00:20:15.590 --> 00:20:18.280
we've introduced this
Wind Down feature.

00:20:18.280 --> 00:20:20.630
So Wind Down sets a
schedule to remind you

00:20:20.630 --> 00:20:23.240
when to switch off at night.

00:20:23.240 --> 00:20:25.250
So you set a bedtime
for your phone,

00:20:25.250 --> 00:20:30.860
and it fades to grayscale and
sets itself to Do Not Disturb.

00:20:30.860 --> 00:20:33.740
So it will silence
notifications.

00:20:33.740 --> 00:20:37.110
It's still completely usable
and allows you to finish up

00:20:37.110 --> 00:20:38.110
whatever you were doing.

00:20:38.110 --> 00:20:40.490
But our studies are showing
that it's a lot less

00:20:40.490 --> 00:20:42.600
engaging and distracting.

00:20:42.600 --> 00:20:45.170
So it's much harder to
get stuck in that sort

00:20:45.170 --> 00:20:52.860
of infinite scroll or a video.

00:20:52.860 --> 00:20:55.550
So that's a important part
of our well-being story.

00:20:58.350 --> 00:21:00.030
It's early days,
and Android will

00:21:00.030 --> 00:21:02.070
continue to invest in
digital well-being,

00:21:02.070 --> 00:21:06.080
both from a consumer and
enterprise perspective.

00:21:06.080 --> 00:21:07.830
Users tell us that
these types of features

00:21:07.830 --> 00:21:10.650
are helping them
to change behavior,

00:21:10.650 --> 00:21:14.070
and it's a real opportunity
for IT departments

00:21:14.070 --> 00:21:16.290
to champion these things
and raise awareness

00:21:16.290 --> 00:21:20.020
with their employees.

00:21:20.020 --> 00:21:22.380
So we'll continue to
invest in well-being

00:21:22.380 --> 00:21:24.720
for consumer and enterprise.

00:21:24.720 --> 00:21:26.790
Something that we're
working on actively

00:21:26.790 --> 00:21:29.393
right now is in-app switching.

00:21:29.393 --> 00:21:30.810
So if you were,
for example, using

00:21:30.810 --> 00:21:33.990
Gmail in your personal profile
and you want to change accounts

00:21:33.990 --> 00:21:38.700
to your work profile to
reply to emails there,

00:21:38.700 --> 00:21:40.200
you can do that
from within the app,

00:21:40.200 --> 00:21:42.420
and it will actually
sort of seamlessly

00:21:42.420 --> 00:21:46.990
switch out the personal and
work version of the app.

00:21:46.990 --> 00:21:48.570
So we're working on that.

00:21:48.570 --> 00:21:52.620
We'll continue to invest
in Android as a platform.

00:21:52.620 --> 00:21:55.730
You may or may not know,
but Android is actually

00:21:55.730 --> 00:22:00.990
a very popular choice for
line-of-business applications,

00:22:00.990 --> 00:22:05.220
so things like kiosks, or
digital signage, point of sale,

00:22:05.220 --> 00:22:07.660
rugged devices.

00:22:07.660 --> 00:22:12.600
So in this sense, Android is
both flexible and powerful.

00:22:12.600 --> 00:22:16.350
And we'll continue to
invest in these things

00:22:16.350 --> 00:22:18.550
on this slide and more.

00:22:18.550 --> 00:22:20.760
So hopefully, this
has given you a feel

00:22:20.760 --> 00:22:23.640
for where Android
is going and why

00:22:23.640 --> 00:22:28.990
Android is a great, great choice
for the needs of workers in IT.

00:22:28.990 --> 00:22:32.970
And we hope that you've
seen how Android devices can

00:22:32.970 --> 00:22:38.930
be both secure and respectful
and is showing how IT needs,

00:22:38.930 --> 00:22:40.910
business needs, and
needs of employees

00:22:40.910 --> 00:22:42.320
can, will be, aligned.

00:22:42.320 --> 00:22:45.670
[MUSIC PLAYING]

